# Parser: Graph Structure Conventions

`packages/parser/src/parser.ts` turns an exported `defineWorkflow(...)` into a `WorkflowGraph` (types in `types.ts`). Supporting a new language construct is a deliberate change: add it here, render it in `packages/ui/src/nodes/`, and cover it in `src/__tests__/`. The same package also produces the execution transform (`execution-transform.ts`) and powers each project's `.catamorphic/scripts/check.ts` (`check.ts`).

## What the parser accepts

- A Workflow is an exported direct `defineWorkflow` call with an inline builder, an inline `steps` array, direct `defineBoundary` / `defineBatch` entries, and inline callbacks. There is no other workflow kind, registry file, or manifest; source is the registry.
- Batch steps (`defineBatchStep` exports) may be called only inside `defineBatch.process`. Anywhere else is a parse error.

## Node types

| Type | Represents | Container |
|------|-----------|-----------|
| `input` | The workflow's start, labeled "Start"; carries `triggerBindings` | No |
| `durable-boundary` | One `defineBoundary` atomic retry scope | Yes |
| `batch` | One `defineBatch` scope: source, per-item process, optional sink | Yes |
| `source` / `sink` | Batch paging and optional bounded sink | No |
| `step` | A call in a boundary or process body (see below) | No |
| `pause` | A returned `pause(...)` transition | No |
| `call-workflow` | A returned `callWorkflow(...)`; inlines the child's scopes | Yes |
| `if-block` | Invisible wrapper around an if/else chain or a ternary | Yes |
| `branch` | One arm of an `if-block` | Yes |
| `loop-block` | `for`, `for...of`, `for...in`, `while` | Yes |
| `parallel-block` | `Promise.all([...])` | Yes |
| `scope-block` | An IIFE or bare `{ }` block | Yes |
| `return` | "Item result" of a batch process body | No |
| `delay` | A call named `sleep(...)` (not a `@catamorphic/workflow` export; durable waits use `pause`) | No |

`WorkflowNodeType` also lists `parallel`, which the parser does not emit. `stage` is never a node type or public concept.

## Steps and host calls

- Any call statement (awaited or not), awaited variable initializer, or returned call inside a boundary or process body becomes a `step`. A `"use step"` function supplies its JSDoc label, icon, description, and parameters.
- Host calls get readable labels instead of code: `context.host["catamorphic.sessions"].deliver` reads "Message a chat", `context.connections.gmail.search` reads "Gmail: search". Add new host operations to `SESSION_OPERATION_LABELS` or `hostCallLabel`.
- A boundary may return a transition or a call directly. A ternary (`cond ? pause(...) : doThing()`) becomes an `if-block` with an "if" and an "else" branch, each drawing its step, host call, `pause`, or `call-workflow`. An else arm that only returns a value is dropped rather than drawn empty.
- Inside a boundary, a `return` ends the flow without a `return` node. Never add synthetic return nodes after `pause` or `callWorkflow`.

## Containers and edges

- Children set `parentId` to their container. Container bodies are parsed with `[]` as `previousIds`, so there is no edge from a container to its first child.
- Root edges connect scopes in `steps` order. Nothing inside a scope connects directly to the next top-level scope.
- **Boundary:** nameless unless its leading JSDoc has `@displayname`; `sourceRange` spans the whole `defineBoundary(...)` call.
- **Batch:** `source` leads into the process nodes; physical batch-step calls are marked with metadata (`batchStep`, `batch:*`), never separate workflow nodes.
- **Call-workflow:** recursively inlines the child workflow's scopes. Detect recursive cycles and stop at the recursive call (`recursiveWorkflow` metadata).
- **if-block:** edges go from the previous node straight to each branch, never to the wrapper. Branches carry `metadata.branchType` (`"if"`, `"else if"`, `"else"`) and a label of the raw condition, or "Otherwise". Without an `else`, when every branch ends in a return, an edge from the previous node bypasses the block so the flow can continue. The canvas prettifies conditions (`prettyCondition` in `packages/ui`); the parser keeps the source text.
- **loop-block:** label `for x of items` (or `loop`). No `loop-body` or `loop-back` edges.
- **parallel-block:** label "Parallel" or a leading `@displayname`. Array elements become `step` children, or `scope-block` children for IIFEs, with no edges between them. Array destructuring registers each binding for provenance.
- **scope-block:** label from a leading `@displayname`, else "Block".

Edge types are `sequential` (default), `branch-true`, `branch-false`, and `parallel`. Removed types, do not re-add: `loop-body`, `loop-back`.

## Graph metadata

- `controls: { cancel: true }` sets the `cancellation` capability. Cancellation is a host Run control, not a node.
- Graphs and discovery results expose `capabilities` (`batchProcessing`, `cancellation`), never a category field.
- Workflow `permissions` (ADR 0158) and trigger config must be constants; hosts read them without running project code.

## Source ranges

Every node has a `sourceRange` (`start`, `end`, `startLine`, `startColumn`, `endLine`, `endColumn`, optional `file`) built with `getSourceRange`. Ranges drive code and canvas linking, so a leaf's range must be the exact call and a container's range must enclose its children. Container and leaf ranges overlap on purpose: linking prefers the smallest leaf and falls back to the container on structural lines.

## JSDoc and provenance

- Tags: `@displayname`, `@description`, `@icon`, and `@param name - @displayname Label | @description Text`. Variables, IIFEs, bare blocks, `Promise.all` statements, boundaries, and batches accept a leading `@displayname`.
- For each step argument, trace the value back to its source variable. `StepArgumentSource` records the variable, its display name, and the producing node's id and label, which powers "where does this value come from?" in the host's inspector.
